A||||5||cmyokley||88 Head light Problem||||8
Z||000000||cmyokley||10-01-1999||04:18 PM||||After fighting the headlight system in my 86GT for two years I have converted my headlights to the system out of an 88. This is by far a much superior system. Only one problem...Without the hood on both headlights Work great, but with the hood down the right headlight does not want to come up all the way. There is no obstruction in the headlight door, although both doors have a lot of spring hold down force. I know the way the electronics work is that the motors will shut off when the resistance reaches a specified number. Could the springs on the door be too strong, could the motor itself be too weak. I did rebuild the motors with new bushings and clean them up etc... will the large screw on the bottom of the motor cause the motor to load differently? What is that screw for and how tight is it suppose to be?<P>Thanks for your help.||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||10-01-1999||09:41 PM||||I have this exact same problem.  My headlight motors are original to the car(1988).  I have not been able to determine what the problem is either.  Doesn't happen all the time, but often enough to get on my nerves (have to get back out of the car and screw around with the twisty knob thing).  Just thought I'd add this so people don't think it's only on rebuilds or something.||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||10-01-1999||11:25 PM||||You could have a motor with a turn in the windings shorted out. A single turn or 2 would be enought to thro the controller way the heck off when even a tiny load is on the motor.<P>BTW... so far my 87's work ok. No work has been done to them at all.||||reg||1
Z||000003||cmyokley||10-05-1999||05:34 PM||||theogre,<P>anything that can be done about a problem like this other than replacing tha motor? anyother ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||10-05-1999||06:55 PM||||It sounds like a bunch of you guys need to do my headlight mod. Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-05-1999||08:05 PM||||if the motor actually has a turn shorted, it needs replacement or rewinding. You need to find out if one motor is drawing more current than another. You need an amp meter for that.<P>try cleaning and lubing all the moving parts. including the slide patches on the bottom of the door. You could have a pivot or slide binding.<P>exactly what did you do when you cleaned the motor?<P>IF the adjustment screw you are asking about is the one I think it is, it adjusts the end play on the motor shaft. if it's too loose or too tight then yes it could cause problems. To tight binds, but to loose lets the armature move around and maybe not get a good contact from the brushes.||||reg||1
